---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump';
const title = 'Postscript vs SMSBump: Shopify Migration Guide (2026)';
const desc = 'SMSBump is decommissioned. Plan a Shopify messaging migration to Postscript while separating retained Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty, consent records and automation ownership.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Postscript or SMSBump better for Shopify SMS?',
    answer: "SMSBump is no longer an active alternative: its login page confirms decommissioning. Evaluate Postscript as a replacement using your recoverable subscriber records, required journeys and retained Yotpo integrations, rather than a historical feature ranking.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can SMSBump replace Postscript entirely?',
    answer: "No. Yotpo scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and the SMS application now reports that its dashboard and background services are inactive. Do not plan a new Postscript-to-SMSBump migration.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Postscript vs SMSBump pricing compare?',
    answer: "Compare a current Postscript proposal with your actual replacement budget, not an old SMSBump price. Separate messaging charges, implementation, number provisioning, support and retained Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty fees. Historical invoices help reconcile spend but are not a current offer.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Yotpo loyalty integration favor SMSBump over Postscript?',
    answer: "Retaining Yotpo Loyalty does not restore SMSBump. Define which tier, balance and reward-expiry events must reach the new sender, then demonstrate the supported integration path and field freshness. A historical native connection does not prove current Postscript parity.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Postscript or SMSBump?',
    answer: "Consider Sequenzy as an email evaluation candidate if you are rebuilding email separately. Keep SMS migration responsibility explicit and compare that split with the Postscript Email offering available to your account; neither choice recovers missing legacy records automatically.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better TCPA compliance tooling?',
    answer: "This guide does not rank legal protection. Reconcile documented opt-ins and suppressions before importing, and demonstrate opt-out handling on the replacement configuration. Software features alone do not establish whether a particular list may be messaged.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles cart abandonment SMS better?',
    answer: "SMSBump is decommissioned, so the task is to rebuild and validate cart recovery in the replacement. Test purchase cancellation, duplicate events, reply ownership and interaction with existing email flows before enabling live sends.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: "Use a proposed loyalty-reward cutover rehearsal: a tier change, a points redemption before send, an opt-out while queued and a duplicate event. Require the expected eligibility and suppression outcomes before approving the replacement. These are proposed checks, not reported test results.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Postscript vs SMSBump</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Migration guide</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs SMSBump for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          SMSBump is retired, so this is a migration decision for Shopify teams with legacy Yotpo messaging records and ongoing Reviews or Loyalty programs. Assess whether Postscript can take over the required SMS journeys, whether email should move too, and which reward data must cross the new boundary. No merchant outcomes or comparative performance tests are claimed here.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Legacy messaging', tool: 'SMSBump', reason: 'Decommissioned; recover records rather than plan new campaigns.' },
            { label: 'Replacement candidate', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Evaluate SMS and account access to its announced Email offering.' },
            { label: 'Retained programs', tool: 'Yotpo Reviews / Loyalty', reason: 'Keep product contracts and event ownership separate from messaging.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>SMSBump (Yotpo SMS)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Current status</td><td>SMS platform with announced Email offering</td><td>Messaging application decommissioned</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Validate replacement scope and access</td><td>Recover existing records where available</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Loyalty data</td><td>Demonstrate supported event and field mapping</td><td>Historical connections are not a live sending option</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost basis</td><td>Current written proposal</td><td>Historical invoices and closure reconciliation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email</td><td>Confirm rollout access and migration scope</td><td>Yotpo Email was also scheduled for retirement</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The retirement changes the decision</h2>
        <p>Yotpo's <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. The <a class="link" href="https://www.smsbump.com/users/login">SMSBump login notice</a> now confirms that the dashboard and associated background services are inactive. This is no longer a comparison between two available SMS subscriptions.</p>
        <p>Yotpo's migration announcement names Attentive and Omnisend as recommended partners. Postscript remains a candidate to evaluate against your own requirements, rather than a replacement endorsed by that announcement. The retirement concerns messaging; it does not mean that a retained Reviews or Loyalty program must be removed. Keep those contracts, widgets, reward rules and owners on a separate worklist.</p>

        <h2>Start with the records you actually have</h2>
        <p>Inventory archived subscriber exports, suppression files, opt-in evidence, flow diagrams, campaign reports and invoices. Record the date and source of each artifact. A phone number in Shopify or a loyalty membership record is not, by itself, evidence of permission to send promotional texts. Quarantine records with unresolved permission or conflicting opt-out status instead of silently treating an import as approval.</p>
        <p>Because the old dashboard is inactive, do not promise a fresh self-service export. Ask the former provider which records remain recoverable and through what process; recovery availability and your account's retention terms remain unresolved until answered. If a saved export predates later opt-outs, reconcile those later records before using it. Keep original files unchanged and document each transformation in the working copy.</p>
        <p>Build a flow inventory with trigger, eligibility, message content, delay, exit condition and current replacement owner. Mark missing branches explicitly. Historical revenue reports may help prioritize reconstruction, but their attribution windows cannot establish that the same results will recur on a different platform.</p>

        <h2>Retain loyalty value without retaining a retired sender</h2>
        <p>For a store keeping Yotpo Loyalty, the useful question is whether the replacement can act on the right reward state. Map customer identifiers, tier, spendable points, redemption status and expiry timestamp to the journey that uses them. Decide whether each value is an event snapshot or a fresh lookup at send time; a cached balance can make an otherwise correct message misleading after redemption.</p>
        <p>Ask the implementer to show the actual supported integration route for your account, including retries, update latency and missing-customer handling. Do not assume either a native connector or middleware is required without that demonstration. Separately identify who sends review requests so a rebuilt post-purchase flow does not duplicate a retained Reviews request.</p>

        <h2>A proposed reward-expiry cutover rehearsal</h2>
        <p>Use controlled test profiles and approved test destinations. Create one eligible reward-expiry event, then redeem the reward before the scheduled message. The proposed acceptance outcome is cancellation or content recalculation, according to the documented policy, rather than a stale expiry warning. Repeat with an opt-out during the delay: the promotional send should remain suppressed.</p>
        <p>Next, deliver the same tier-change event twice and deliver an older balance update after a newer one. Check that the system does not issue duplicate early-access invitations or overwrite current reward state with the older value. Record the event identifiers, timestamps, expected state and actual result; a successful import alone does not establish these behaviors.</p>
        <p>Finally, reply with a reward question and confirm who receives it, what happens outside staffed hours and whether a human can see the relevant reward context. These checks are proposed acceptance work, not experiments performed for this article. If a required reward field cannot be kept current, launch the simpler eligible SMS journeys first and keep that reward message disabled.</p>

        <h2>Postscript now has an email decision too</h2>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Postscript Email page</a> advertises its email offering and invites merchants to get on the list. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.prweb.com/releases/postscript-launches-email-on-a-new-ai-native-retention-marketing-platform-302884359.html">September 2026 company announcement</a> describes rollout beginning with existing customers. Confirm access, onboarding date and the migration work included for your account before retiring an existing email sender.</p>
        <p>You can evaluate consolidating email and SMS, or keep email with a separate provider while replacing only SMS. For either architecture, assign one owner to each cart, welcome and reward journey. Rebuild purchase exits and channel suppressions explicitly; sharing a customer profile does not by itself prove that two independently scheduled messages will coordinate.</p>

        <h2>Budget for reconstruction, not a historical bundle</h2>
        <p>Request a written quote for the actual channel mix, destinations, message types, expected segments, carrier charges, support and implementation work. Keep retained Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty costs separate. Savings from closing a retired messaging line cannot be counted again as savings from switching between two active vendors.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical implementation budget: reconstructing six reward-related journeys at three hours each plus four hours of data reconciliation requires 22 hours. At an assumed internal rate of $50 per hour, that is $1,100 of internal work, before supplier fees. This is planning arithmetic, not a vendor quote or observed merchant cost. Replace the assumptions with your inventory and include unresolved recovery work as a separate estimate.</p>

        <h2>Where Sequenzy fits</h2>
        <p>If email needs a separate rebuild, include Sequenzy in the email evaluation alongside the Postscript offering accessible to your account. Bring the same welcome, post-purchase and reward-message requirements to that evaluation. Do not infer automatic Yotpo data recovery, equivalent loyalty integrations or production SMS replacement from an email product recommendation. Require a demonstrated event path and clear SMS ownership before choosing a split stack.</p>

        <h2>Approve a replacement against a recoverable scope</h2>
        <p>Choose Postscript only after confirming the offered account scope, resolving usable consent records and demonstrating the journeys you intend to activate. Keep recovery gaps and unsupported reward paths visible in the launch decision. A working replacement can preserve a separate Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty program; it cannot make SMSBump an active alternative again.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo vs Yotpo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/recart-vs-postscript">Recart vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email apps for jewelry stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs SMSBump FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
